there's a great story uh told by nelson  mandela which i think is a good lesson  you're asking for a specific tip um uh  he was actually born the son of a tribal  chief and when asked by a journalist  you know uh how did you become a great  leader and as an aside  nelson mandela is a very important case  study because different personalities  are viewed differently depending on  where you go in the world but nelson  mandela  regardless of where you go in the world  is universally regarded as a great  leader so he's an important case for  for for folks like me um uh and he was  asked  by a journalist how did you learn to  become a great leader and he said  when i was a boy i remember two things  i remember going to tribal meetings with  my father  and i remember uh that he they always  sat in a circle  and my father was always the last to  speak  um and if you think he's the most senior  person in the room he's this tribal  chief and he was always the last to  speak  you know we're constantly telling people  you have to be a better listener but at  the end of the day we have to speak  eventually and when we communicate  but i think the practice of being the  last to speak when you're in a meeting  we do this all the time the senior  person comes and goes okay  even well-intentioned you know this is  the problem this is the challenge we  have this is what i think we should do  but i'm interested in what you have to  say well it's too late you buy us the  room it's over  or you've made people feel that their  opinion doesn't matter and so for  leaders to practice  listening and this is a and by the way  i'm tired of the term soft skills  hard skills are the skills we need to  learn to do our jobs and human skills  are the learn  of the skills we need to be better human  beings and it's the combination of hard  skills and human skills  that make us very good and listening is  one of those so i think  be coming into a meeting stating a  problem not giving an opinion  not playing poker not letting anybody  know what you think as they speak  asking questions only out of curiosity  not to you know push somebody in one  direction or another  and literally be the last to speak not  only do you make people feel  like their opinions matter but you get  the benefit of of new ideas  you can still make the same decision you  wanted to make at the beginning but you  get all that additional  benefit so practice being the last to  speak that's a great lesson
 

